---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import EarlyAccessForm from '../components/EarlyAccessForm.astro'
import { softwareApplication } from '../schema'

const title = 'Features | Klaroly booking app for makeup artists'
const description =
  'Enquiries, a clash-aware calendar, rate cards, digital wedding makeup contracts, invoices, deposits, automated emails and client notes. See what Klaroly does.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={[softwareApplication()]}>
  <PageHeader title="What Klaroly does">
    A tour of the parts. Everything here is being built now, and the order is
    being set by the artists we are talking to.
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="prose max-w-2xl">
    <h2>Enquiries</h2>
    <p>
      Most bookings start as a message read while your hands are full. Klaroly
      gives enquiries their own place instead of leaving them in an inbox.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Add an enquiry in a few taps: name, date, venue, how they found you</li>
      <li>It goes on the calendar as a soft hold straight away</li>
      <li>
        Clash warnings cover enquiries, not just confirmed bookings, so you know
        a date is getting busy before you promise it to anybody
      </li>
      <li>Turn an enquiry into a booking without typing any of it again</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Bookings and the calendar</h2>
    <p>One booking holds the whole job.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The client, their contact details, and anybody else involved</li>
      <li>Trial date and wedding date, with times and call times</li>
      <li>The venue, with travel time and distance worked out for you</li>
      <li>What was agreed, what it costs, what has been paid</li>
      <li>List view or calendar view, whichever suits the moment</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quotes and your rate card</h2>
    <p>Set your prices once and stop working them out on a calculator.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A line for every service you offer: bride, bridesmaids, mother of the
        bride, children, gents, whatever you call yours
      </li>
      <li>
        Travel, early starts and hotel nights as their own lines, because they
        are their own costs
      </li>
      <li>
        Build a quote on your phone in a few taps and send it before you have
        got back to the car
      </li>
      <li>Override any price on any booking when a job is not standard</li>
      <li>
        Prices are recorded on the booking as they were agreed, so raising your
        rates next year does not quietly rewrite last year
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Contracts</h2>
    <p>The part most artists put off.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A proper agreement, with your terms and the booking details filled in</li>
      <li>Sent as a link, nothing to download or install</li>
      <li>They read it, type their full name, tick the box, submit</li>
      <li>
        Both sides get a signed PDF immediately, and a confirmation email goes
        out automatically
      </li>
      <li>
        A full record of when it was sent, opened and signed, kept in case it is
        ever needed
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Invoices, deposits and balances</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Deposit on booking, balance before the day, which is how most artists
        already work
      </li>
      <li>Generate an invoice from the booking, no re-typing</li>
      <li>Klaroly tracks what is outstanding and how close the date is</li>
      <li>Reminders go out on their own until it is paid, and stop when it is</li>
      <li>
        Record a bank transfer in one tap, because most clients will pay that way
      </li>
      <li>
        Agreed to wait? Snooze the reminders without pretending the invoice is
        paid
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Emails that send themselves</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Enquiry acknowledgements, booking confirmations, trial reminders, balance
        chasers
      </li>
      <li>Written once, in your words, then left to run</li>
      <li>
        Everything sent is logged against the booking, so you can see what went
        out and when
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Client notes and photos</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Notes as a dated stream, so you can see how a booking developed</li>
      <li>Skin type, sensitivities, the look they want</li>
      <li>
        Trial photos and wedding photos at full resolution, kept with the booking
      </li>
      <li>Add a reminder to any note</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>On your phone, and at your desk</h2>
    <p>
      Klaroly is one product with three faces: an iPhone app, an Android app,
      and a desktop version in the browser. The same information in all three.
    </p>
    <p>
      The phone is for the ninety per cent that is tap, tap, done. The desktop
      is for setting up your templates and doing your month end, which nobody
      wants to do with their thumbs.
    </p>

    <h2>What is coming next</h2>
    <p>
      We are building the booking, contract and invoice side first. After that:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Card payments.</strong> Take a deposit or a balance by card, straight
        from the invoice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deeper automations.</strong> More of the messages you send by hand,
        sent for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hair, and the rest of the wedding morning.</strong> Other people who
        turn up with a car full of kit have close to the same problem.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Early access members get a say in the order.</p>

    <h2>What we are deliberately not doing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a directory.</strong> Klaroly does not send you clients and does
        not rank you against other artists. It is your tool, not a marketplace.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a form builder.</strong> You will never be asked to design a form.
        Sections turn on and off, that is all.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not something your clients log into.</strong> They get a link when they
        need one. Nobody is asked to create an account to book a face.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <EarlyAccessForm id="features" class="mt-2" />
</BaseLayout>
